<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<pretext xmlns:ns0="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <ns0:include href="../docinfo.ptx" />
  <article>
    <section xml:id="ps13-section">
      <title>Problem Set 13</title>
      <subtitle>Power Series</subtitle>
      <ns0:include href="ps01-tr.ptx" xpointer="xpointer(//section[1]/introduction)" />
      <exercises>
        <exercise xml:id="mpl-2025-11-15-derivs-of-arctan">
          <statement>
            <p> Let <m>f(x) = \tan^{-1}(3x)</m>. Use a Taylor series to find <m>f^{(2025)}(0)</m>. </p>
          </statement>
          </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-Rogawski-11-7-51">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Express <m>\int_0^1 e^{-x^3}\,dx</m> as the sum of a series.
              Approximate its value to within an error of at most <m>10^{-4}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-Rogawski-11-7-678">
          <introduction>
            <p>Let <me>f(x) = \frac{1}{(1-x)(1-2x)}</me></p>
          </introduction>
          <task xml:id="ex-Rogawski-11-7-67">
            <statement>
              <p>
                Use the partial fraction decomposition to find the Maclaurin series for <m>f(x)</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>
          <task xml:id="ex-Rogawski-11-7-68">
            <statement>
              <p>
                  Find the Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m> centered at <m>a=2</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
              <p>
                Use the same partial fraction decomposition, and substitute <m> x = 2 + (x-2) </m>.
              </p>
            </hint>
            </task>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="Rogawski-11-7-83">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Use Taylor series to evaluate
              <me> \lim_{x\to 0} \left(\frac{\sin x^2}{x^4} - \frac{\cos{x}}{x^2}\right)</me>. 
              Compare this to the same limit evaluated using L'Hôpital's Rule.
            </p>
          </statement>
          </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-Rogawski-11-7-84">
          <statement>
            <p>
              In this exercise we will prove that the Maclaurin series for 
              <m>f(x) = ln(1+x)</m> converges to <m>f(1)</m> at <m>x=1</m>.
              This allows us to find the sum of the alternating harmonic series.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Show that for <em>all</em> <m>x\neq 1</m> and all
                natural numbers <m>N</m> that:
                <me>
                  \frac{1}{1+x} = \sum_{n=0}^N(-1)^nx^n + \frac{(-1)^{N+1}x^{N+1}}{1+x}
                </me>
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Integrate from <m>x=0</m> to <m>1</m> to obtain:
                <me>
                  \sum_{n=1}^{N+1} \frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n} 
                  = \ln 2 + (-1)^N \int_0^1 \frac{x^{N+1}}{1+x}\,dx
                </me>
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Show that the integral on the right-hand side is smaller than
                <m>\frac{1}{N+2}</m> in absolute value. 
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Conclude that the infinite series on the left-hand side converges to <m>\ln 2</m>:
                <me>
                  \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n} = \ln 2.
                </me>
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>          
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-Rogawski-11-7-91">
          <title>
            Irrationality of <m>e</m>
          </title>
          <introduction>
            <p>
              We will show that <m>e</m> is irrational using a proof by contradiction.
              Suppose that <m> e=M/N</m>, where <m>M</m> and <m>N</m> are positive integers.
            </p>
          </introduction>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Show that <m>M!/e</m> is an integer.
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Use the Maclaurin series for <m>e^x</m> evaluated at <m>x=-1</m> 
                to show that there exists an integer <m>B</m> such that
                <me>
                  \frac{M!}{e} = B + (-1)^{M+1}\left(\frac{1}{M+1} - \frac{1}{(M+1)(M+2)} + \cdots \right)
                </me>
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>
          <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Use what you know about sums of alternating series with decreasing terms
                to show that <m>\left|M!/e - B\right|</m> is an integer strictly between <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>.
                Contradiction!
              </p>
            </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
      </exercises>
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>